---
// Ask Tally: how to connect an AI assistant to Tally's read-only MCP
// connector at /mcp (worker/mcp.ts). Static, no script.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import '../styles/ask-tally.css';

const URL_MCP = 'https://tally.milux.co.uk/mcp';
---
<Base title="Ask Tally from your AI assistant: Tally" description="Connect Claude or another AI assistant to Tally, the open directory of UK defence capability, and ask it questions in your own words.">
  <div class="ask-tally">
    <header class="site-header">
      <p><a href="/">Back to the Tally map</a></p>
      <h1>Ask Tally from your AI assistant</h1>
      <p>Tally has a connector that any AI assistant supporting the Model Context Protocol (MCP) can use. Add it once, then ask your assistant things like "who does counter-drone work within 25 miles of Portsmouth?" and it will look the answer up in Tally, with links back to each company on the map.</p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="url-heading">
      <h2 id="url-heading">The connector address</h2>
      <p>Use this address wherever your assistant asks for a remote MCP server URL:</p>
      <p><code class="connect-url">{URL_MCP}</code></p>
      <p>There is nothing to sign up for and no key. It is free to use.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="claude-heading">
      <h2 id="claude-heading">Add it in Claude</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>In Claude on the web or the desktop app, open <strong>Customize</strong>, then <strong>Connectors</strong>.</li>
        <li>Choose <strong>+ Add</strong>, then <strong>Add custom connector</strong>.</li>
        <li>Paste <code>{URL_MCP}</code> and choose <strong>Continue</strong>.</li>
        <li>For <strong>Authentication</strong>, choose <strong>No sign in</strong>, then choose <strong>Add</strong>.</li>
        <li>In a chat, choose <strong>+</strong>, then <strong>Connectors</strong>, and turn Tally on. Then ask your question.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>For Claude Team and Enterprise owners</h3>
      <p>An owner can add Tally for everyone in the organisation: open <strong>Organization settings</strong>, then <strong>Connectors</strong>, choose <strong>Add</strong>, then <strong>Custom</strong>, then <strong>Web</strong>, and paste the address above. Members then turn it on in a chat as above.</p>
      <h3>Claude Code</h3>
      <p>Run <code>claude mcp add --transport http tally {URL_MCP}</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="other-heading">
      <h2 id="other-heading">Add it in other MCP clients</h2>
      <p>Any client that supports remote MCP servers over Streamable HTTP can use Tally. Add a server with the address above, the HTTP (Streamable HTTP) transport and no authentication. Look for a setting called connectors, tools, integrations or MCP servers.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="tools-heading">
      <h2 id="tools-heading">What your assistant can do</h2>
      <p>The connector is read-only. It offers four tools:</p>
      <dl>
        <dt><code>search_companies</code></dt>
        <dd>Find companies by capability or capability group, region, size, accreditation, framework, membership, regional cluster or keyword, and near a UK postcode or a point within a radius. Returns up to 25 companies at a time with a summary, capabilities, location and a link to the company on the Tally map.</dd>
        <dt><code>get_company</code></dt>
        <dd>The public profile Tally holds for one company.</dd>
        <dt><code>list_capabilities</code></dt>
        <dd>Tally's grouped capability taxonomy and the accepted values for the other filters.</dd>
        <dt><code>list_clusters_and_events</code></dt>
        <dd>The UK's regional defence and security clusters and their upcoming events. Members-only events are shown only as a date and a link to join the cluster.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="data-heading">
      <h2 id="data-heading">What it uses and what it keeps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Answers come only from the same public data the Tally map shows. Your assistant's own model does the thinking; Tally runs no AI model for the connector.</li>
        <li>Tally does not log or keep what your assistant asks. To apply a fair-use limit, it counts requests for one minute by the network address they come from and, when your assistant sends one, the connection id Tally gave it. Those counts are held in memory only and are not stored.</li>
        <li>Postcodes are placed from Tally's own data. If Tally cannot place one, your assistant can give a latitude and longitude instead.</li>
        <li>The limit is generous for normal use. If you hit it, wait a minute and try again.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Something wrong or missing? Please <a href="https://milux.co.uk/contact/">get in touch</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
